<body>
<main class="app">
  <section class="calc">
    <header class="brand">
      <span class="brand-mark" id="brandMark"></span>
      Babylonian Calculator
      <span class="tabs">
        <button id="tabCalc" class="viewtab on">Calculator</button>
        <button id="tabConvert" class="viewtab">Convert</button>
      </span>
      <button id="themeBtn" class="ghost" title="Toggle light / dark">&#9789;</button>
      <button id="helpBtn" class="ghost" title="How to use">? help</button>
    </header>

    <div id="calcView">
    <div class="display">
      <div id="cuneiEcho" class="cunei" aria-label="current number in cuneiform"></div>
      <div id="expr" class="expr empty"></div>
      <div class="result-row">
        <div id="result" class="result"></div>
        <button id="copyBtn" class="copy-btn" title="Copy result" aria-label="Copy result">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
               stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12" rx="2"/>
            <path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10"/>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div id="convertView" hidden>
      <div class="conv-cats" id="convCats" aria-label="unit category"></div>
      <div class="conv-grid">
        <div class="conv-amt-disp" title="tap the digit pad below — each tap is one base-60 place">
          <span id="convAmtDisp">0</span>
          <span id="convAmtDec"></span>
          <span class="conv-entry-keys">
            <button id="convRadix" class="ghost-key" title="radix point — start fractions (2;30 = 2.5)">;</button>
            <button id="convDel" class="ghost-key" title="delete">&#9003;</button>
            <button id="convClr" class="ghost-key" title="clear">C</button>
          </span>
        </div>
        <select id="convFrom" class="conv-sel" aria-label="from unit"></select>
        <button id="convSwap" class="conv-swap" title="Swap units">&#8646;</button>
        <select id="convTo" class="conv-sel" aria-label="to unit"></select>
      </div>
      <div class="conv-result">
        <button id="convRateNote" class="conv-note" hidden title="Refresh exchange rates"></button>
        <div class="result-row">
          <div id="convOut" class="conv-out"></div>
          <button id="convCopy" class="copy-btn" title="Copy result" aria-label="Copy result">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
                 stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12" rx="2"/>
              <path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </div>
        <div id="convCunei" class="cunei conv-cunei" aria-label="result in cuneiform"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="padZone" class="controls">
      <div id="digitPad" class="digits" aria-label="sexagesimal digit keys"></div>
      <aside class="rail">
        <div class="toggles">
          <button id="angleBtn" class="tgl on">DEG</button>
          <button id="styleBtn" class="tgl" title="Cycle result notation">94</button>
        </div>
        <div id="sciPad" class="sci" aria-label="scientific keys"></div>
        <div id="opPad" class="ops" aria-label="operator keys"></div>
        <button id="eqBtn" class="eq">=</button>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="side">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>History <button id="clearHist" class="ghost">clear</button></h2>
      <ol id="history"></ol>
      <div id="histEmpty" class="empty-note">No calculations yet.<br>Tap a digit to begin.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <details class="legend">
        <summary>About these numerals</summary>
        <p>~4000 years ago Babylonians wrote all numbers in base&nbsp;60 — the system
        behind our 60-minute hour and 360-degree circle. A vertical wedge is&nbsp;1,
        a corner wedge (𒌋) is&nbsp;10; ones stack in rows of three after the tens.
        They had no zero, so an empty place is marked with a pair of slanted wedges.
        The calculator uses the authentic Unicode cuneiform number signs when your
        device has a cuneiform font, and draws them otherwise.</p>

        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Positional, like ours.</b> Each place is worth 60× the one to its right,
            exactly as decimal places are worth 10×. The digits themselves run from
            <span class="num">1</span> to <span class="num">59</span>.</li>
          <li><b>Two signs build every digit.</b> A vertical wedge = 1, the corner wedge
            𒌋 = 10. So <b>𒌋𒌋𒐂</b> reads 20 + 4 = <span class="num">24</span>; unit
            wedges never exceed three per row — <span class="num">9</span> (𒐇) stacks
            in three rows.</li>
          <li><b>Places are read left to right</b>, most significant first:
            <span class="num">1,25</span> means 1×60 + 25 = <span class="num">85</span>.
            Tap the digit pad here the same way: tap <span class="num">1</span> then
            <span class="num">25</span> and you'll see <span class="num">1,25</span>.</li>
          <li><b>Fractions worked the same way.</b> Places right of the radix point count
            sixtieths: <span class="num">0;30</span> = 30/60 = one half. Babylonian
            scribes did division by multiplying by reciprocals — handy with so many
            exact fractions available.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Why base 60?</h3>
        <p>Sixty is divisible by 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20 and 30 — more than any
        smaller number. Halves, thirds, quarters and fifths all come out exact, which made
        trade, land measurement and astronomy painless. The Sumerians started counting in
        sixties around 3000&nbsp;BCE; Old Babylonian scribes (~1900–1600&nbsp;BCE) turned it
        into the fully positional system this calculator mimics. "Cuneiform" just means
        <i>wedge-shaped</i> — scribes pressed reed styluses into wet clay tablets that
        then dried nearly indestructible, which is why millions of their homework sheets
        still survive.</p>

        <h3>The missing zero</h3>
        <p>For centuries an empty place was simply left blank, so "1,1" (3600+1) could be
        misread as "11". Later scribes began filling gaps with two slanted wedges — the
        world's earliest <i>placeholder</i> zero. It was never treated as a number you can
        calculate with; that idea had to wait for Indian mathematicians over two thousand
        years later. The dashed key on the pad plays the same role.</p>

        <h3>The legacy on your clock</h3>
        <p>When Greek astronomers adopted Babylonian data, they kept the sixtieths:
        a degree split into 60 <i>partes minutae primae</i> ("first small parts") and again
        into <i>secundae</i>. That's literally where <b>minutes</b> and <b>seconds</b>
        come from — on maps and on your watch face. This app's °′″ and h&nbsp;m&nbsp;s
        result modes write numbers exactly the way those astronomers would have.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<div id="helpOverlay" class="overlay" hidden>
  <div class="help-card">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <table>
